from <math> import sqrt, pnjlim, jexp, vcrit_of, q_dep, rb_of;

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  NPN Gummel-Poon Bipolar Junction Transistor
//
//  Replaces the previous Ebers-Moll model. Ebers-Moll is the special case of
//  this one: set Vaf = Var = 0 (infinite Early voltage), Ikf = Ikr = 0 (no
//  high-injection knee), Ise = Isc = 0 (no non-ideal base current), Nf = Nr =
//  1, and Rb = Re = Rc -> 0, and the equations below collapse term by term
//  into Is/Bf*(e^(vbe/Vt) - 1) and friends. Everything GP adds is an
//  enhancement on top of that skeleton, which is why the terminal topology —
//  two base junction sources plus one transport source — is unchanged.
//
//  What GP buys over Ebers-Moll, in rough order of how much it changes a
//  simulation:
//
//    Vaf/Var   Early effect. Ebers-Moll has ro = infinity, so a common-emitter
//              stage reports gain of exactly -Rc/(re + Re) with no ro||Rc
//              correction and an ideal current-source collector. This is the
//              term most likely to change an answer you already have.
//    Ikf/Ikr   High-injection roll-off. Beta falls at high Ic instead of
//              staying flat forever, so a saturating or hard-driven stage
//              stops reporting impossible currents.
//    Ise/Ne    Non-ideal (recombination) base current. Beta falls at LOW Ic
//    Isc/Nc    too — the low end of the Gummel plot, which Ebers-Moll misses
//              entirely and which sets the noise floor of a small-signal stage.
//    Rb/Re/Rc  Ohmic terminal resistance. Re in particular is local series
//              feedback that shows up directly in gain, and Rb sets the input
//              impedance a base drive actually sees.
//    Rbm/Irb   Base-resistance modulation. Current crowds toward the emitter
//              edge as injection rises, so Rb falls from RB toward RBM. Only
//              matters once the base current approaches IRB, which for a
//              small-signal device is well into its switching range.
//    Tf/Tr     Diffusion charge. Tf sets fT and so the small-signal bandwidth;
//              Tr is the saturation storage charge, which is what makes a
//              hard-driven switch slow to turn OFF. With the previous model's
//              fixed 10p/4p caps a saturated stage came out of saturation in
//              tens of ns; with Tr it takes hundreds, which is the difference
//              between a switching-loss figure that is believable and one that
//              is off by a decade.
//    Cj*/Vj*/  Bias-dependent depletion charge, replacing fixed capacitors.
//    Mj*/Fc    A B-C capacitance that falls from 22p to 8p as the collector
//              swings out to -10 V is the difference between a Miller pole
//              that moves with the operating point and one that does not.
//
//  ── Per-iteration limiting (manual 13.1) ────────────────────────────────────
//
//  Junction limiting is math's pnjlim(), fed from nr_prev(). pnjlim() takes
//  the previous iterate as an ordinary argument — it cannot see iteration
//  history by itself — and nr_prev() is exactly the thing that reports it:
//
//      pnjlim(V(bi) - V(ei), nr_prev(V(bi) - V(ei)), nvt_f, vcrit)
//
//  It bounds the STEP, not the device. At convergence the step is small and
//  the limiter is in pass-through, so the answer sits on the true exponential
//  I-V. Each junction is limited once, on its ideal Is/nvt, and the resulting
//  voltage feeds both the ideal and the leakage (Ise/Isc) term — the leakage
//  diode is the softer of the two, so the stiffer one sets the bound.
//
//  (pnjlim() itself was rewritten to the Berkeley formulation; the previous
//  implementation turned a large downward step into an upward jump to vcrit.
//  See its own header in math/physicalmath.hvr.)
//
//  ── Convergence: the solver is real Newton, so keep derivatives alive ───────
//
//  It is easy to look at stamp_current_source (runtime/mna/matrices.cpp),
//  see that it writes only B_static and nothing into G, and conclude there is
//  no device Jacobian and hence no true Newton. That conclusion is wrong, and
//  getting it wrong costs a great deal.
//
//  vm_compute_jacobian (runtime/vm/vm.cpp) builds a FULL numerical Jacobian:
//  it perturbs every node voltage by sqrt(eps)*max(|v|,1), re-runs the analog
//  blocks, and measures the resulting change in B_static. Every dI/dV this
//  model produces is therefore captured, whether or not anything was stamped
//  into G. The solvers (bdf2, ndf2, trapezoidal) consume it with Jacobian
//  reuse and Newton damping modelled on MATLAB's ode15s.
//
//  The practical consequence governs how this model is written: the Jacobian
//  is measured from THIS SOURCE, so anything here with zero slope becomes a
//  zero column in the Jacobian, and a zero column is a singular direction
//  Newton cannot step along. A hard clamp is precisely that. An earlier
//  version of this file carried a derived backstop clamp on vbe/vbc; it did
//  not aid convergence, it caused it to fail — with the clamp removed, cases
//  that had been diagnosed as "physically marginal" converge immediately, and
//  the Gummel plot extends a further decade (beta rolls off to 106 at 317 mA
//  instead of the curve freezing at 199 mA where the clamp bound).
//
//  So: no clamps on the junction voltages. The only bound left is inside
//  jexp(), on the exponential's ARGUMENT, purely to stop a wild first-
//  iteration guess reaching inf. It is deliberately set far outside any
//  physical bias, and everything below it is smooth.
//
//  Rb/Re/Rc remain worth having for physical reasons — Re is series feedback,
//  Rb sets the input impedance a base drive sees — and they do condition the
//  internal nodes. But they are not load-bearing for convergence the way an
//  earlier version of this comment claimed. Do not pass zero for them all the
//  same: R<0> is a literal short, and for Rb a zero-valued synthesized
//  resistor is the same thing, collapsing the internal node onto its terminal.
//
//  ── Charge storage: real, via ddt() ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  The B-E and B-C charges are the full Gummel-Poon ones — depletion charge
//  with the (1 - v/Vj)^-Mj law and Fc forward-bias linearization, plus Tf/Tr
//  diffusion charge — differentiated with ddt() and injected as current.
//  This is the Verilog-A idiom (I = ddt(Q)) and it works here because ddt()
//  is not numerical differentiation: the elaborator rewrites ddt(x) into a
//  1 H inductor fed by a current source carrying x, so KCL forces the
//  inductor's current to x and its terminal voltage to L*dx/dt = dx/dt. The
//  derivative therefore comes out of the MNA solve itself and inherits
//  whatever order and stability the active solver has (macros.go,
//  processAnalogDdt).
//
//  Two consequences worth knowing:
//
//  ddt() is a top-level construct. processAnalogDdt walks only expression,
//  assignment and local-declaration statements in the module body — it does
//  not descend into if-blocks — so a ddt() call must sit at the body's top
//  level. That is why the branchy part of the charge (the Fc seam) lives
//  inside q_dep() and only the finished charge is handed to ddt().
//
//  Nothing about the charge current lands in G, since it arrives as a current
//  source. That would matter for convergence if the capacitive conductance
//  were comparable to the node's, but at Cje ~ 22p and dt ~ 1u it is C/dt ~
//  2e-5 S against the internal base's 1/Rb = 0.1 S — four orders down, so the
//  ohmic resistances dominate here exactly as they do for the DC currents.
//  Cjs stays a plain linear C<> because a substrate capacitance has no bias
//  dependence modelled anyway, and a linear cap in G costs nothing.
//
//  Not modelled: the Xtf/Itf/Vtf transit-time modulation (Tf is taken
//  constant, so fT does not fall off at high current the way a real device's
//  does) and the excess-phase parameter Ptf.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


analog module NPN<double Is,  double Nf,  double Nr,
                  double Bf,  double Br,
                  double Vaf, double Var,
                  double Ikf, double Ikr,
                  double Ise, double Ne,
                  double Isc, double Nc,
                  double Rb,  double Rbm, double Irb,
                  double Re,  double Rc,
                  double Cje, double Vje, double Mje,
                  double Cjc, double Vjc, double Mjc,
                  double Fc,  double Tf,  double Tr,
                  double Cjs, double Vt>()
    [wire base, wire collector, wire emitter]
{
    // Internal (intrinsic) nodes. The GP equations are written at the junction
    // itself, so every V() read below is an internal node — the ohmic drop
    // between terminal and junction is carried by the three resistors, which
    // is exactly what makes Re series feedback rather than a fitting fudge.
    wire bi, ci, ei;

    // Rc and Re are genuinely constant, so they stay ordinary resistors and
    // their conductance lands in G. Rb is current-dependent and is synthesized
    // further down, once the base current it depends on has been computed.
    R<Rc>() [collector, ci];
    R<Re>() [emitter,   ei];

    double nvt_f = Nf * Vt;
    double nvt_r = Nr * Vt;

    // Per-iteration junction limiting (manual 13.1). nr_prev() supplies the
    // previous NR iterate; on the first iteration of a timestep it returns
    // that step's starting guess, so no limiting is applied and the cold start
    // behaves as an unlimited evaluation — intended.
    //
    // Each junction is limited ONCE, on its ideal Is/nvt, and the resulting
    // voltage feeds both the ideal and the leakage (Ise/Isc) term. The leakage
    // diode is the softer of the two, so the stiffer one sets the bound.
    double vbe = pnjlim(V(bi) - V(ei), nr_prev(V(bi) - V(ei)),
                      nvt_f, vcrit_of(Is, nvt_f));
    double vbc = pnjlim(V(bi) - V(ci), nr_prev(V(bi) - V(ci)),
                      nvt_r, vcrit_of(Is, nvt_r));


    // ── Ideal transport currents ────────────────────────────────────────────
    double i_f = Is * (jexp(vbe, nvt_f) - 1.0);
    double i_r = Is * (jexp(vbc, nvt_r) - 1.0);

    // ── Base charge factor qb ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    // q1 is the Early (base-width modulation) term, q2 the high-injection one.
    // Vaf/Var/Ikf/Ikr = 0 means "infinite" in SPICE convention, i.e. effect
    // disabled, which is how the Ebers-Moll degenerate case is selected.
    double inv_vaf = 0.0;
    double inv_var = 0.0;
    if (Vaf > 0.0) { inv_vaf = 1.0 / Vaf; }
    if (Var > 0.0) { inv_var = 1.0 / Var; }

    // Forward Early effect is modulation by the COLLECTOR-base voltage, so vbc
    // pairs with Vaf and vbe with Var — not the other way round.
    double denom = 1.0 - vbc * inv_vaf - vbe * inv_var;
    if (denom < 0.1) { denom = 0.1; }   // caps q1 at 10; deep reverse only
    double q1 = 1.0 / denom;

    double q2 = 0.0;
    if (Ikf > 0.0) { q2 = q2 + i_f / Ikf; }
    if (Ikr > 0.0) { q2 = q2 + i_r / Ikr; }
    if (q2 < 0.0)  { q2 = 0.0; }        // i_f, i_r reach -Is in reverse bias

    double qb = 0.5 * q1 * (1.0 + sqrt(1.0 + 4.0 * q2));

    // ── Terminal currents ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Transport current, thinned by the base charge: this single division is
    // where both the Early effect and high-injection roll-off enter Ic.
    double i_ct = (i_f - i_r) / qb;

    // Base current: ideal component (transport/beta, unaffected by qb) plus
    // the non-ideal recombination component that dominates at low bias.
    double i_be = i_f / Bf + Ise * (jexp(vbe, Ne * Vt) - 1.0);
    double i_bc = i_r / Br + Isc * (jexp(vbc, Nc * Vt) - 1.0);

    current_source<>(i_be) [bi, ei];
    current_source<>(i_bc) [bi, ci];
    current_source<>(i_ct) [ci, ei];

    // ── Base resistance, synthesized ────────────────────────────────────────
    // R<> takes a compile-time constant, so a current-dependent Rb cannot be a
    // resistor primitive. It is built instead from a voltage source carrying
    // its own branch current times the wanted resistance — V = R*I is the
    // defining equation of a resistor, so the solver reproduces one exactly
    // (verified against a real R<> to nine digits).
    //
    // I(rbx) and not nr_prev(I(rbx)): I() already returns the latest solved
    // iterate, which is what a fixed point wants. nr_prev() would reach one
    // iteration further back, and that extra lag is purely destabilizing here
    // — with it, this construction fails to converge outright once the
    // synthesized resistance approaches the rest of the loop in size.
    double ib_tot = i_be + i_bc;
    double rb_eff = rb_of(Rb, Rbm, Irb, ib_tot, qb);
    double v_rb   = rb_eff * I(rbx);
    voltage_source rbx<>(v_rb) [base, bi];

    // ── Charge storage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Total junction charge: diffusion (carriers in transit, proportional to
    // the transport current) plus depletion (the space-charge layer). The
    // diffusion term is what makes fT finite; the depletion term dominates in
    // cutoff and sets Miller feedthrough.
    //
    // These two statements must stay at the module body's top level — see the
    // header on processAnalogDdt's walk. The charges are deliberately built
    // from the LIMITED vbe/vbc and from i_f/i_r, so charge and conduction are
    // always evaluated at the same operating point.
    // The ddt() results are bound to locals rather than written inline into
    // the current_source port lists: a primitive's () argument must be a plain
    // signal name, and processAnalogDdt only rewrites expression, assignment
    // and local-declaration statements anyway.
    double q_be  = Tf * i_f + q_dep(vbe, Cje, Vje, Mje, Fc);
    double q_bc  = Tr * i_r + q_dep(vbc, Cjc, Vjc, Mjc, Fc);
    double i_cbe = ddt(q_be);
    double i_cbc = ddt(q_bc);

    current_source<>(i_cbe) [bi, ei];
    current_source<>(i_cbc) [bi, ci];

    // Substrate capacitance stays linear: no bias dependence is modelled for
    // it, and a constant cap costs nothing to stamp straight into G.
    C<Cjs>() [ci, ei];
}
